@startuml wizard
title tinytuya Setup Wizard — how it obtains local_key

actor User
participant "Smart Life /\nTuya Smart App" as App
participant "tinytuya wizard\n(python -m tinytuya wizard)" as Wizard
participant "LAN scanner\n(scanner.devices)" as Scan
participant "Tuya IoT Cloud\n(iot.tuya.com API)" as Cloud
database "devices.json\n(+ snapshot.json,\ntuya-raw.json)" as Files

== Prerequisites (one-time, manual) ==
User -> App : Pair every bulb\n(non-guest account)
note right of App
  A device that is not paired
  cannot be fetched later.
end note
User -> Cloud : Create developer account,\ncreate Cloud project
User -> Cloud : Link Tuya App account\n(QR scan, "Automatic",\n"Read Only Status")
Cloud --> User : API ID (Access ID),\nAPI Secret (Access Secret),\nData-center Region

== Running the wizard ==
User -> Wizard : run wizard
Wizard -> User : prompt API ID / Secret / Region
User --> Wizard : credentials

Wizard -> Scan : discover LAN devices (optional)
note right of Scan
  UDP 6666 / 6667 / 7000
  yields IP + Device ID + version,
  NOT the key.
end note
Scan --> Wizard : [ {ip, id, version} ... ]

Wizard -> Cloud : sign request with API ID+Secret\n-> get access token
Cloud --> Wizard : access token
Wizard -> Cloud : GET device registry for the\nlinked app account
Cloud --> Wizard : [ {name, id, key(local_key), ...} ]

Wizard -> Wizard : merge cloud registry\nwith LAN scan (match by id)
Wizard -> Files : write devices.json\n(name + id + ip + key + version)
Files --> User : local_key now available offline

== After the wizard ==
User -> User : paste id / ip / key into\nyour own tool (e.g. notuya-gui)
note over User
  From here communication is
  LAN-only: AES with local_key
  over TCP 6668. No cloud needed
  until keys are re-issued.
end note

@enduml
